:: day1_npc_queen_message [nobr]
<h3>The Queen's Message</h3>

<p>A knock at the door. Not Rowan's knock — softer, more formal. I consider not answering. The door opens a crack — a handmaiden, young, wearing Silverveil grey rather than palace livery. She doesn't enter. She extends a hand through the gap. A sealed parchment. The royal seal — silver stag, crescent moon.</p>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">HANDMAIDEN</span>"From the Queen, my lady."</div>

<p><em>My lady.</em> The first time anyone has addressed me with a feminine honorific. The words hit my eardrums and detonate somewhere behind my sternum. I take the note. The handmaiden curtsies — I see her knees bend through the gap — and retreats.</p>

<p>I break the seal. Mother's handwriting — the elegant, controlled script of a woman who was taught penmanship as a form of statecraft.</p>

<p><em>Rest. I'll come to you tomorrow. I love you.</em></p>

<p>Seven words. Eight, if you count contractions honestly. And every one of them is a blade and a bandage simultaneously.</p>

<p><em>Rest.</em> Because she knows I won't. Because she knows I'm lying in this room cataloguing every wrong thing about this body and rest is the last thing I'm capable of. The instruction is not advice — it's a mother's magic, the kind that doesn't work but is cast anyway because the alternative is silence.</p>

<p><em>I'll come to you tomorrow.</em> Not today. Today she's giving me the gift of not being seen. Today she knows that being looked at by the woman who asked me to do this would be too much. She's managing me. She's always been managing me. She's doing it from a sickbed with blood in her lungs and she's still better at it than anyone else alive.</p>

<p><em>I love you.</em> Three words that don't change anything and change everything. Three words that are true regardless of the body they're delivered to, regardless of the name attached to that body, regardless of the jaw shape and the chest size and the presence or absence of a cock.</p>

<p>I crumple the note. Throw it at the wall. Watch it bounce off the white stone and land on the floor, a small wadded ball of parchment and maternal love.</p>

<p>Then I pick it up. Smooth it. Read it again. Put it under the pillow.</p>

<<set $d1f_npc_queen_message to true>>
<<set $stress += 5>>
<<set $stress -= 5>>
<<set $affSeraphina += 2>>
<<addItem "queens_letter" "The Queen's Letter" "Rest. I'll come to you tomorrow. I love you.">>

